vmhal9x + vmhal3d (JHRobotics/vmhal9x 8bc480e): a z-buffer cleared with DirectDraw
(Blt DDBLT_DEPTHFILL - Dungeon Keeper 2 does it every frame instead of a Direct3D clear) was
only filled in VRAM; GL keeps its own depth buffer, which was never cleared, so every
depth-tested triangle failed (black world, only the cursor hand visible). Blt32 now passes
each depth fill (surface, value, rectangle) to vmhal3d (new export hal3d_DepthFill32, through
the existing USER_LIB bridge, only when vmhal3d is already loaded); the context rendering
with that z-buffer clears its GL depth at its next draw (MesaApplyDepthFill, from
MesaBufferTexturesChanges). Found 2026-10-31. Apply after vmhal3d-rowlength.patch.

--- a/vmhal9x.h
+++ b/vmhal9x.h
@@ -119,6 +119,19 @@
 
 BOOL __stdcall D3DHALCreateDriver(DWORD *lplpGlobal, DWORD *lplpHALCallbacks, LPDDHAL_DDEXEBUFCALLBACKS lpHALExeBufCallbacks, VMDAHAL_D3DCAPS_t *lpHALFlags);
 
+/* DDBLT_DEPTHFILL of a z-buffer, passed from Blt32 to vmhal3d (hal3d_DepthFill32): GL keeps
+ * its own depth buffer, which a fill of the surface in VRAM alone does not clear */
+typedef struct _hal3d_depthfill_t
+{
+	DWORD flat;  /* fpVidMem of the filled z-buffer */
+	DWORD value; /* fill value, shifted down to the z bits */
+	DWORD max;   /* largest z value of the format */
+	RECT  rect;
+	BOOL  full;
+} hal3d_depthfill_t;
+
+void D3DDepthFill(hal3d_depthfill_t *df);
+
 /* FBHDA */
 BOOL FBHDA_load_ex(VMDAHAL_t *pHal);
 
